{"name":"Disappearing Island","hex":"#bbdee5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"disappearing","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of disappear"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["disappearance"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/disappearing"},{"term":"island","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A contiguous area of land, smaller than a continent, totally surrounded by water.","A contiguous area of land, smaller than a continent, partially surrounded by water; a peninsula; a half-island."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To surround with water; make into an island.","To set, dot (as if) with islands."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/island"}],"entries":[{"title":"French Frigate Shoals","blocks":[{"text":"The French Frigate Shoals is the largest atoll in the Northwestern Hawaiian Islands, located about 487 nautical miles northwest of Honolulu. Its name commemorates French explorer Jean-François de Galaup, comte de Lapérouse, who nearly lost two frigates when attempting to navigate the shoals. It consists of a 20-mile-long (32 km) crescent-shaped reef, twelve sandbars, and the 120-foot-high (37 m) La Perouse Pinnacle, the only remnant of its volcanic origins. The total land area of the islets is 61.508 acres (24.891 ha), while the total coral reef area of the shoals is over 232,000 acres (94,000 ha). Tern Island, with an area of 26.014 acres (10.527 ha), has a landing strip and permanent habitations for a small number of people. It is maintained as a field station in the Hawaiian Islands National Wildlife Refuge by the United States Fish and Wildlife Service. East and Trig, the two largest islands after Tern, have mostly washed away. The islands are surrounded by a coral reef, and the islands themselves are noted breeding ground for turtles, seals, and birds. Habitation is difficult with little vegetation and no fresh water. The French Frigate Shoals are also called Lalo as part of the marine nature reserve, and along with other islands are visited periodically for research. The highest point in the shoals is on La Perouse Pinnacle, which is a volcanic rock that rises 122 ft (37 m) above the surrounding lagoon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/FFS_ISS006_37912.jpg/330px-FFS_ISS006_37912.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_Frigate_Shoals"},{"title":"Island","blocks":[{"text":"An island or isle is a piece of land, distinct from a continent, completely surrounded by water. There are continental islands, which were formed by being split from a continent by plate tectonics, and oceanic islands, which have never been part of a continent. Oceanic islands can be formed from volcanic activity, grow into atolls from coral reefs, and form from sediment along shorelines, creating barrier islands. River islands can also form from sediment and debris in rivers. Artificial islands are those made by humans, including small rocky outcroppings built out of lagoons and large-scale land reclamation projects used for development."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Hawaje-NoRedLine.jpg/330px-Hawaje-NoRedLine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Island"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/FFS_La_Perouse.jpg/330px-FFS_La_Perouse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/FFS_La_Perouse.jpg/330px-FFS_La_Perouse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The towering La Pérouse Pinnacle is made of hard volcanic rock and rises 122 ft (37 m)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFFS_La_Perouse.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Hawaiian_monk_seal_at_French_Frigate_Shoals_01.jpg/500px-Hawaiian_monk_seal_at_French_Frigate_Shoals_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Hawaiian_monk_seal_at_French_Frigate_Shoals_01.jpg/1280px-Hawaiian_monk_seal_at_French_Frigate_Shoals_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Hawaiian_monk_seal_at_French_Frigate_Shoals_01.jpg/500px-Hawaiian_monk_seal_at_French_Frigate_Shoals_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Monk seal and noddy terns at Tern Island, French Frigate Shoals","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHawaiian_monk_seal_at_French_Frigate_Shoals_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/PMNM_-_Birds_on_French_Frigate_Shoals_%2827369203434%29.jpg/500px-PMNM_-_Birds_on_French_Frigate_Shoals_%2827369203434%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/PMNM_-_Birds_on_French_Frigate_Shoals_%2827369203434%29.jpg/1280px-PMNM_-_Birds_on_French_Frigate_Shoals_%2827369203434%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/PMNM_-_Birds_on_French_Frigate_Shoals_%2827369203434%29.jpg/500px-PMNM_-_Birds_on_French_Frigate_Shoals_%2827369203434%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Red-footed Booby on French Frigate Shoals","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APMNM_-_Birds_on_French_Frigate_Shoals_(27369203434).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Starr-990526-0856-Sporobolus_pyramidatus-habitat-Tern_Island-French_Frigate_Shoals_%2824444666371%29.jpg/500px-Starr-990526-0856-Sporobolu